Day 268/300: Jump Game

Problem Statement #55

Problem Statement Solution:

You are given an integer array nums. You are initially positioned at the array's first index, and each element in the array represents your maximum jump length at that position.
Return true if you can reach the last index, or false otherwise.

Solution Approach:

The goal is to determine if the last index is reachable. 
Instead of exploring every possible jump (Backtracking) or storing sub-problem results (Dynamic Programming), we use a Greedy approach to track the furthest reachable index in a single pass.

Step-by-Step Logic:
Initialize a variable farthest to 0.
Iterate through the array using a pointer i.
If at any point i > farthest, it means the current index is unreachable; return false.
Update farthest at each step: farthest = max(farthest, i + nums[i]).
If farthest reaches or exceeds the last index (nums.length - 1), return true early.

Here's the code:

class Solution {
    public boolean canJump(int[] nums) {
        int farthest = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (i > farthest) return false;
            farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
            if (farthest >= nums.length - 1) return true;
        }
        return true;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity:	O(n)
Space Complexity:	O(1)
